Reflection on Sensing the World Unit 

This week in our Sensing the World module we worked to better understand and apply the techniques to manufacture microchips. We begun learning about the process of photolithography: applying a photo-reactive compound on the surface of a material. Exposing it to a specific wavelength of light through a mask called a reticle, causing some of the photo-reactive compound to dissolve. This allows us to deposit a thin layer of metal in the shape of the remaining photo-reactive compound and the reticle. The process allows for absurdly small and precise parts. Then we toured the facilities around campus that make this possible. First, we toured the clean room, donning white suits and shoe slips, where we learned about how the photoresist is evenly applied to a wafer of silicon in a centrifuge. We also learned how chips are checked for defects and the importance of keeping the room free of any particles as we saw the damage even a human hair could inflict on a chip. I asked a question about the purpose of the yellow lights and we learned that higher energy light, even in the visible spectrum could expose the photoresist before the lithography process. Finally, we traveled to the materials lab where we saw the machines that make the single gallium nitride crystals used to make semiconducting wafers. 
In the practical portion of this unit we attempted to make an interdigitated electrode or IDE using lithography. The purpose of these was to sense water vapor in the air. The idea being that if enough water gathered between the digits of the electrode it would conduct and we could read a changing resistance across the circuit.  We cut out the shape of our mask on vinyl sheets using a cutting device at Wilbur powerhouse. We used these to apply a layer of electrically conductive ink, scraping the remainder off with a squeegee. This was supposed to guarantee the dimensions of the IDE as the layer would be no thicker than the vinyl sheet. After removing the vinyl mask we, using a centrifuge, applied PEDOT, an organic polymer that can conduct electricity and absorb water. Once it dried, the PEDOT me measured a resistance across the IDE. Then we blew on the IDE and the humidity of our breath was absorbed by the PEDOT and changed the resistance of our IDE. Although it did work, our resistance readings were incredibly inconsistent and unreliable. Our dimension measurements were also poor as I had to cut out the mask for our IDE by hand. We also delved into circuits building a simple one with 4 leds, a coin battery and copper tape. We used the copper tape to make an outline of the butterfly’s wings, the antenna folding over to hit the top of the battery and start the circuit. 
Other than what was previously mentioned, I also learned that nitrogen is obtained from ammonia and used as the atmosphere for the growing of Gallium nitride crystals. The nitrogen is obtained this way because it requires too much energy to break the tripled bonded nitrogen gas. In both facilities we visited, I was shocked to see the number of gas sensors and gas lines traveling throughout. I imagine that must be quite the headache to keep those systems organized and sealed. In the lab, we struggled to get a satisfactory IDE and even the ones that worked failed to conduct. Either way I improved my abilities working with lab partners and namely, using a scalpel. 
	The last two classes we opened github and use copilot to launch our own website about ourselves. Finally, we begun to practice coding an arduino. Although I could not plug the device into my computer I still practiced writing the code on the arduino interface. 
